WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.259
Measure power draw at idle, startup, model load, prompt processing, and sustained generation.

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:16.020
Capacity planning includes GPU, CPU, drives, fans, power conversion, room cooling, circuit limits, and future hardware.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:26.284
Monitor GPU memory, utilization, temperature, clocks, power, errors, fan speed, host memory, disk, queue depth, latency, and process health.

00:00:26.284 --> 00:00:31.040
Per-device visibility helps reveal imbalance and stalled work.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:41.803
Logs should preserve model, runtime, driver, device assignment, split, context, batch, request outcome, and failure without retaining sensitive prompts unnecessarily.

00:00:41.803 --> 00:00:45.558
Configuration records make performance changes explainable.

00:00:45.558 --> 00:00:48.812
Crash recovery needs defined behavior.

00:00:48.812 --> 00:00:57.574
Decide whether services restart, requests retry, models reload on remaining devices, or the system fails closed.

00:00:57.574 --> 00:01:02.830
Test recovery before assuming one extra GPU provides redundancy.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:11.592
Physical setup requires secure cards, supported cables, clear airflow, stable mounting, safe power, and restricted access.

00:01:11.592 --> 00:01:18.851
High-temperature surfaces, spinning fans, heavy cards, and improvised open frames require appropriate controls.

00:01:18.851 --> 00:01:29.114
Maintenance includes dust removal, filter inspection, cable and fan checks, thermal review, driver patching, storage cleanup, and benchmark regression.

00:01:29.114 --> 00:01:34.371
Operational reliability matters more than a one-time record score.

00:01:34.371 --> 00:01:38.626
An extra GPU is not automatic redundancy.

00:01:38.626 --> 00:01:46.887
A shared power supply, host, storage device, driver, or runtime can still stop every workload.

00:01:46.887 --> 00:01:51.142
Test component and service failure behavior explicitly.
